## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical design choices for low-latency allocators in multi-core, NUMA contexts, including cache locality, lock-free synchronization, and teardown safety.

## Key Concepts

- Per-core caches for locality
- Global lock-free freelist for cross-core refills
- Batch operations to amortize synchronization cost
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo: per-core cache node and refill logic
typedef struct Object { struct Object *next; } Object;
typedef struct { _Atomic(Object*) head; } FreeList;

#define BATCH 64

Object* allocate(FreeList *local, FreeList *global){
    Object* head = atomic_load_explicit(&local->head, memory_order_acquire);
    if(head){
        local->head = atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&local->head, NULL, memory_order_acquire);
        return head;
    }
    // refill from global (simplified)
    for(int i=0;i<BATCH;i++){
        Object* o = pop_global(global);
        if(!o) break;
        push_local(local, o);
    }
    return allocate(local, global);
}

void deallocate(FreeList *local, Object *o){
    push_local(local, o);
}
```

